---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page, same shape and same rules as
 * stronglifts-5x5-tracker.astro and fitnotes-alternative.astro. Read the
 * StrongLifts file's header before editing this one; the reasoning is
 * identical.
 *
 * This page was deliberately PARKED until iOS shipped (spec section 4 of
 * badger-fit docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md, "at
 * the iOS launch moment"), because until 2026-09-09 the honest answer to
 * "FitNotes on iPhone" ended at "there isn't one, and nor is there a Badger".
 * It is built now because Badger is on the App Store.
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: FitNotes is someone else's app. Everything here
 * is phrased as compatibility (Badger reads the backup file their app writes),
 * never as affiliation or endorsement, and never as a criticism of their app.
 * The searcher arriving here LIKES FitNotes - that is why they typed its name -
 * so a page that opens by running it down loses them in one sentence. Do not
 * reword the title or h1 into anything that reads as "FitNotes for iPhone",
 * which would claim to BE their app on a platform they do not ship to.
 *
 * Division of labor with the sibling page, so neither grows into the other:
 * /guides/fitnotes-alternative/ owns the full account of what the import
 * carries. THIS page owns the phone-switch mechanics, which is the part that
 * page has no reason to cover: getting the file off an Android phone you may be
 * about to sell, and onto an iPhone. The "what comes across" section here is a
 * deliberate summary that links there rather than repeating the table.
 *
 * Claims checked against the app source on 2026-09-12, app version 1.1.11:
 *   - no iOS release of FitNotes exists (external fact, and the reason for the
 *     page; nothing in our code to check)
 *   - the parser and importer have NO platform branch at all: no Platform.is,
 *     no defaultTargetPlatform anywhere under lib/features/import/. The file is
 *     read with dart:io File and sqflite, both of which are first-class on iOS,
 *     so the iPhone import is the same code path as the Android one
 *     (fitnotes_parser.dart, fitnotes_importer.dart)
 *   - the picker is FileType.any, which on iOS is the system Files picker, so
 *     any location Files can see works (fitnotes_import_screen.dart:75)
 *   - in-app path: Settings, Your data, Import from FitNotes
 *     (settings_screen.dart:1084, under the "Your data" section header), and
 *     the same import is offered in first-launch onboarding
 *     (onboarding_screen.dart:339)
 *   - re-importing merges rather than duplicating (fitnotes_importer.dart)
 *   - iPhone only, iOS 15.0 or later: TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY = 1 and
 *     IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET = 15.0 in ios/Runner.xcodeproj, which matches
 *     what the App Store listing's own Compatibility section says
 * Note for a future editor: the app's own settings row calls the FitNotes file
 * "a FitNotes CSV export", which is wrong - it is a SQLite database. Do not
 * copy that wording onto the site to match; the site is right and the app row
 * is the thing to fix.
 */

const description =
  'FitNotes is an Android app and has no iPhone version. Here is what to do with the log you already have: Badger is on the App Store and reads the .fitnotes backup file directly.';

const modified = '2026-09-12';
---

<HelpLayout title="FitNotes on iPhone" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="FitNotes on iPhone"
    description={description}
    dateModified={modified}
    about={['FitNotes', 'iPhone', 'Workout tracking']}
    breadcrumb={[
      { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' },
      { name: 'Switching to Badger', url: '/guides/switching/' },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>FitNotes on iPhone</h1>

  <p>
    The short answer is that there is no iPhone version. FitNotes is an Android app and always has
    been, so switching phones means picking something else to log in. That is an annoying answer, and
    the useful part is what happens next, because the years of training in it do not have to stay on
    the old phone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-log">Your log can come with you</h2>

  <p>
    FitNotes backs up to a <code>.fitnotes</code> file, and that file is a real database rather than
    a flattened export. Badger reads it directly, on the phone, with nothing uploaded anywhere, and
    Badger is on the App Store. So the move is three steps: back up while you still have the Android
    phone, get the file onto the iPhone, import it.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is the same import code on both platforms, not an Android-only feature with an iPhone excuse
    attached. Nothing in it branches on which phone you are holding.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>On the Android phone, before you let go of it.</strong> In FitNotes, go to
      <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup</strong> and write a <code>.fitnotes</code> file. Do this before
      you wipe or trade in the phone. Apple's switch-to-iPhone tools move photos, contacts, messages
      and the like, but not a third-party Android app's saved data, and there is no iPhone FitNotes
      for it to land in even if they did.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Move the file to the iPhone.</strong> Email it to yourself, or drop it in iCloud Drive,
      Google Drive, Dropbox or OneDrive, or move it with a computer and a cable. AirDrop is not an
      option here, since it only talks to other Apple devices. Anything that ends with the file
      reachable in the iPhone's Files app will do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Install Badger</strong> from the App Store.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Import.</strong> Go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from
      FitNotes</strong> and pick the file. It is the standard iOS file picker, so browse to wherever
      the file landed. The same import is offered during first-launch onboarding if you would rather
      do it straight away.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the preview</strong> before you commit: exercises, workout days and sets. This is
      also where you tell Badger which unit the backup holds. It assumes kilograms, which is what
      FitNotes normally stores, so switch it to pounds only if yours does not. An older backup, from
      before FitNotes changed its database layout around 2018, carries the unit per set and needs no
      answer from you. Then import.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    It runs more than once safely. Sets landing on a date you already have merge into that session
    and nothing already logged is overwritten, so if you find an older backup later you can add it
    without making a mess. Step-by-step detail is in
    <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing-fitnotes">importing from FitNotes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-comes">What comes across</h2>

  <p>
    Because the backup is a database, considerably more survives the move than the sets alone:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Your categories, keeping the colors you gave them.</li>
    <li>Your exercise library, landing back in those categories rather than in an "Imported" bucket.</li>
    <li>Every set, with its date, weight, reps, distance, time and any comment on it.</li>
    <li>Your body weight history, and your custom measurements with their units.</li>
    <li>Your records, worked out again from the history once it lands, so the graphs start out full.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    What does not: warmups as warmups, because FitNotes does not mark them and nothing can guess
    retroactively, and routines, which are a separate thing you set up in Badger. The full account,
    including the older pre-2018 backup layout and how pounds stay pounds, is on
    <a href="/guides/fitnotes-alternative/">moving to Badger from FitNotes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-youre-getting">What you are switching to</h2>

  <p>
    Worth saying plainly, because a page that only talks about the import is selling you the door
    rather than the room. Badger is a training log with tools attached, not a coach that runs your
    program for you. If you liked FitNotes for being fast, free, offline and uninterested in your
    email address, that is the same list Badger was built against: no account, no sign-in, no
    subscription, no ads, and no sync you did not ask for.
  </p>

  <p>
    On top of that you get routines that pre-fill each session with your last weights,
    <a href="/programs/">programs that ship built in</a>, per-machine and per-gym records, a plate
    calculator and a warmup generator that work from the plates your gym actually owns, a rest timer
    that starts itself, and <a href="/help/progress/">graphs worth opening</a>. And you can leave
    again whenever you like: every set exports to CSV, and the whole database backs up to a
    <a href="/badger-format/">documented file format</a> that is ordinary readable text. Neither is a
    paid feature. <a href="/promise/">The promise</a> is that written down where you can hold us to
    it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">What it needs</h2>

  <p>
    An iPhone running iOS 15 or later. Badger is an iPhone app only: there is no iPad build, because
    it has never been designed or tested for that screen and shipping it untested would be worse than
    leaving it out. It is free, with no paid tier to graduate to, which
    <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">the no-subscription guide</a> spells out
    against the trackers people usually weigh up.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you end up with a phone on each platform, or move back later, Badger is on Google Play too and
    its own backup file carries the whole database between them.
    <a href="/help/phone-migration/">Moving to a new phone</a> covers that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    FitNotes is the work of its own developer, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by,
    sponsored by, or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility and nothing else:
    Badger reads the backup file that their app writes. FitNotes is a good app, and the only thing
    wrong with it here is that it does not run on the phone you are holding.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The section this page sits in, named above the h1 so a reader landing here
     from a search knows there are sibling pages for the other apps. Same as
     fitnotes-alternative.astro. */
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* Matches the sibling pages: a disclaimer should read as one rather than
     competing with the guidance above it. Muted is AA-checked in both themes. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
